## Implemented boundary

Fresh scratchDir allocations record their actual native owner PID, random
process token, directory identity (device/inode/birth identity) and explicit
retention. The public writeScratchOwnershipManifest stays a non-adopting legacy
marker writer and never supplies purge authority. Retention enabled after
allocation is also persisted while preserving the registered owner. Unknown,
legacy, foreign, changed or unreadable metadata stays retained. Global keep is
checked before selecting candidates and again before removal.

A dead PID alone is insufficient: the allocator must have issued an actual
registered cleanup request. The identity/token-bound request sidecar is written
by removeScratchDir before its native removal call. This preserves the accepted
224 ownership manifest/error contract and restricts reclamation to cleanup the
owner already requested. An abandoned allocation without that request remains
unknown, including possible unsettled child/controller resources. Multi-process
controller resources are not adopted by this mechanism. Live/PID-reused owners
remain retained; liveness checks use process.kill(pid,0), never a spawned shell.

The purge binds the exact ownership and request bytes and directory identity,
then rechecks those, retention, containment and native owner absence immediately
before rmSync without an asynchronous gap. It does not follow directory links
or linked descendants. Age is a secondary eligibility filter, not settlement.
Legacy records are never upgraded or inferred safe. These are bounded native
checks, not a claim of atomic filesystem operations against a hostile concurrent
writer.

The numeric compatibility function still returns only the actual number of
confirmed removals. New purgeStaleVitestTempDetailed distinguishes removed paths
from retained paths/reasons. Unreadable candidate metadata retains the original
cause in that report and emits a visible warning while unrelated work remains
runnable. An unreadable root throws its native error. Actual removal or absence
verification failures throw AggregateError with original causes and the partial
report; they never count as successful cleanup, never trigger another cleaner,
and do not stop inspection of other eligible owned candidates. There is no
automatic replay of an uncertain removal or an inverse operation.

